 * ============================================================================================
 *  PROVISIONAL DRAFT -- GATE F. NOT ACTIVE. NOT RECONCILED AGAINST PRODUCTION.
 * ============================================================================================
 *
 * Held out of the live migration path the same two independent ways the other drafts here are:
 * an undiscovered directory, AND a `*.php.draft` name that cannot match Laravel's `*_*.php` glob.
 * tests/Unit/Database/MigrationHygieneTest.php asserts both. Read README.md before promoting.
 *
 * ============================================================================================
 *  THIS ONE IS DIFFERENT FROM THE OTHER EIGHT, AND THE DIFFERENCE MATTERS
 * ============================================================================================
 *
 * The other drafts here RECONSTRUCT tables that live code already reads and writes -- every
 * column is an inference from `$fillable`, and "the dump wins on every disagreement".
 *
 * THIS TABLE DOES NOT EXIST ANYWHERE. No code writes it, no production database has it, there is
 * nothing to reconcile against and no dump can contradict it. It is a NEW table for a NEW
 * concept, specified by the owner's decision of 2026-10-24:
 *
 *     "Require a separate verified cash collection event, recording the collector (merchant,
 *      courier or delivery company), collection amount, time and supporting evidence."
 *
 * So its columns are not guesses. Each is one of the four things that sentence names, plus the
 * keys that attach an event to an order and a store. It still sits behind GATE F because D15
 * gates ALL production migrations on verified backups and a restoration rehearsal, not because
 * its shape is uncertain.
 *
 * ============================================================================================
 *  WHY EACH COLUMN IS WHAT IT IS
 * ============================================================================================
 *
 *   collector_type   The decision names exactly three collectors, so this is a short constrained
 *                    string and not free text. An unrecognised fourth value is a mistake, not a
 *                    new kind of collector, and CodCollectionService refuses it before the write.
 *   collector_id     WHICH merchant, courier or delivery company. Deliberately NOT a foreign key:
 *                    the three collector types live in three different tables (`users`,
 *                    `delivery_boys`, and a delivery-company table that does not exist yet), so a
 *                    single FK cannot express it. The integrity rule is enforced in the service.
 *   amount           decimal(18,4), the project's standing money type. NOT nullable: an event
 *                    with no amount is not a collection.
 *   collected_at     WHEN THE CASH CHANGED HANDS, which is not `created_at`. They differ whenever
 *                    an event is entered after the fact, which is the normal case for a courier
 *                    round, and reconciliation keys on this one. Indexed with store_id for the
 *                    "what was collected in this store last week" query this exists to answer.
 *   evidence         Free text, NOT NULL. A photo reference, a signature id, a receipt number,
 *                    whatever the store uses. What counts as sufficient is the owner's to define
 *                    per store; that it EXISTS is enforced.
 *   recorded_by      The user who entered the event -- separate from collector_id ON PURPOSE. The
 *                    person who types it in is often not the person who took the money, and
 *                    collapsing the two destroys exactly the collector-liability trail the
 *                    decision asks to keep. Nullable: a future API or courier app may record an
 *                    event with no signed-in user behind it.
 *
 * ONE EVENT PER ORDER, enforced by a unique index on order_id. A second collection against the
 * same order is either a duplicate or a partial-collection record, and partial collection is a
 * concept this product does not have yet -- so it must be refused at the schema rather than
 * written as if it were a second full settlement. When partial collection is designed, THIS INDEX
 * is the line that has to change, which is why it is called out here.
 *
 * ADDITIVE AND NON-DESTRUCTIVE, per the same decision: this creates a table and alters nothing.
 * No existing row is read, rewritten or back-filled. COD orders already marked Paid by the
 * withdrawn Delivered trigger keep that status and acquire no invented event.
 *
 * ORDERING. Promote this BEFORE or WITH `store_order_financials` (draft 0004). The financial
 * record is meant to follow a collection; creating the financial table first leaves settlement
 * with no verifiable trigger and re-opens the question the 2026-10-24 decision closed.
 */

